---
/*
  SOLVET ET COAGULA. The one home of imagery (2026-08-04).

  The gallery dissolved into this page (DESIGN-PLAN section 65): its fifteen
  artistic frames and the eight process frames descend it together, so
  photographs-as-photographs live here and products only ever live in the shop.
  The menu says Process, because the formula is
  illegible to anyone who does not already know the brand; the formula is THIS
  page's title, inside, in the one display size (section 67).

  THE ORDER, UNWOVEN (2026-08-11, section 85, and it reopens section 65).

  The eight process frames are a NARRATIVE — pattern, cut, dye, dry, build, the
  parts, the finished garments — and section 65's weave dealt up to two artistic
  frames into every gap between them: pattern, two artistic, cut, two artistic,
  dye. So the sequence existed in the data and was unreadable on the page, which
  is the fault a proposal to make this page scroll-driven uncovered. The page did
  not need a mechanism. It needed its own order back.

  So: the eight making frames run CONSECUTIVELY, in the order the work happens,
  and the artistic frames follow as a CODA. Nothing is removed — every artistic
  frame still appears, in its own order, the gallery's dark-to-light descent —
  and nothing is added: no captions, no words, no motion. The story is told by
  sequence alone, which is the only way this page has ever been willing to tell
  anything.

  What section 65 was right about is untouched: the gallery is dissolved,
  photographs-as-photographs live here, and products live only in the shop.
  What it got wrong was interleaving two sequences that each mean something in
  their own order and mean nothing shuffled together.

  NO CAPTIONS, still. The only text is the formula and his own making lines.
  The page invents nothing.

  Portraits pair at desktop, exactly as the gallery did and for the same
  measured reason (section 56): a single full-bleed portrait at 1440 is a
  letterbox that destroys the photograph.
*/
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Mosaic from "../../components/Mosaic.astro";
import {noteBlock, productBlock, weaveMosaic, zip, type MosaicBlock} from "../../lib/mosaic";
import PairedSurface from "../../components/PairedSurface.astro";
import Statement from "../../components/Statement.astro";
import TextSurface from "../../components/TextSurface.astro";
import {getGallery, getSiteSettings} from "../../lib/content";
import type {MediaItem} from "../../lib/media";
import {LOCALES, localePath, pick, toLocale} from "../../lib/locales";
import {imageUrl} from "../../lib/image";
import {isMarked, voiceState} from "../../lib/voice";
import {t} from "../../i18n/ui";
import {SHOW_UNAPPROVED_MARKS} from "../../lib/marks";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return LOCALES.map((lang) => ({params: {lang}}));
}

const locale = toLocale(Astro.params.lang);
const [settings, gallery] = await Promise.all([getSiteSettings(), getGallery()]);

const process = settings.processMedia ?? [];
const artistic = gallery.flatMap((piece) => piece.media ?? []);

/*
  THE WORK FIRST, IN ITS OWN ORDER, THEN THE PHOTOGRAPHS. Two sequences, each
  kept whole, rather than one shuffled out of two. This replaces the deal that
  put up to two artistic frames between every stage of the making.
*/
const sequence: MediaItem[] = [...process, ...artistic];

/*
  THE PAIRED FRAMES ARE THIS PAGE'S OWN (section 82), AND THEY COME OUT OF THE
  CODA, NOT OUT OF THE WORK (section 85).

  Two constraints meet here and the second one is new.

  `makingMedia` is a SUBSET of `processMedia`, so anchoring a composition on a
  frame home already uses puts the same photograph at the head of a composition
  on two pages. That was section 82's rule and it still holds.

  But an anchor is LIFTED OUT of the descending sequence and shown at the top of
  the page, so anchoring on a making frame would punch a hole in the very
  narrative section 85 just restored — pattern, cut, then dye missing because it
  is up there beside a paragraph. So the anchors are taken from the ARTISTIC
  frames, which are a descent rather than a sequence and lose nothing by giving
  up two, and all eight making frames stay consecutive and in work order.

  It also reads better than it did: the stages passage is now directly above the
  eight frames it describes, with nothing between them.

  Falls back to any frame home does not use, and then to the sequence itself, so
  a dataset with no artistic frames still renders a composed page.

  IT IS ONE ANCHOR NOW, NOT TWO (2026-08-13, section 108). The page's two
  passages became one column, so it lifts one photograph out of the descent
  instead of two — and the frame that used to sit beside the stages paragraph is
  back in the mosaic, at full bleed, where it is a photograph rather than a
  margin.
*/
const homeRefs = new Set(
  (settings.makingMedia ?? []).map((m) => m.poster?.asset?._ref).filter(Boolean),
);
const unusedByHome = (items: MediaItem[]) =>
  items.filter((m) => !homeRefs.has(m.poster?.asset?._ref));
const anchors = unusedByHome(artistic);
const fallback = unusedByHome(sequence);
/*
  HIS CHOICE FIRST (2026-08-18, section 128). The rule below still works and
  still runs, but it chooses from whatever happens to be spare, which is not a
  decision anybody made. `processPairMedia` in the studio wins when it is set.
*/
const [chosen] = anchors.length >= 1 ? anchors : fallback.length >= 1 ? fallback : sequence;
const pairA = settings.processPairMedia ?? chosen;
const mosaic = sequence.filter((m) => m.poster?.asset?._ref !== pairA?.poster?.asset?._ref);

const [first] = sequence;
const making = pick(settings.makingStatement, locale);
/*
  The stages text is OURS in every language, so it is a draft rather than a
  translation and says so wherever marks are shown.
*/
const processText = pick(settings.processText, locale);
const processIsMarked = SHOW_UNAPPROVED_MARKS && Boolean(processText);
const notice = "draftNotice";

const voice = voiceState(settings, locale);

/*
  THE MIXED ROW, AND THIS IS THE PAGE THE REFERENCE BUILDS IT ON (2026-08-12,
  section 116).

  `RO28` and `RO40` are both from Rick Owens' PROCESS page, and both put a
  garment cut out on pale ground into a row with prose and a wide process
  photograph. That is the row this page could not build until the third kind of
  image existed.

  THE CUT-OUTS ARE ALL HERE NOW, BAR THE HOME ONE (2026-08-17, section 123,
  the OWNER's call). They were shared three ways on 2026-08-13 — one to the
  home two-up, two to /about, three to here — and /about's two have come over,
  so this page takes five of the six.

  It is the right page for them and the sharing was the mistake. A cut-out is a
  garment lifted off its ground and laid on pale paper: it is a way of showing
  the WORK. /about is about where the brand and its material come from, and a
  figure floating on white says nothing about either. Spreading them evenly
  treated six photographs as a resource to divide rather than as a kind of
  picture that belongs somewhere.

  Where they land inside the run is `weaveMosaic`'s business — see lib/mosaic.ts
  for why the slots are 1, 3, 6 and not anywhere.
*/
const cutouts = (settings.cutoutMedia ?? []).filter((c) => c.media?.poster);
const mine = cutouts.slice(1);

/*
  AND THE PROSE GOES IN WITH THEM (2026-08-13, section 118), which is the last
  structural difference between this page and the reference. `Mosaic` has
  carried a `note` block since it was written and no page passed one, so the
  page ran pictures-only for a dozen screens while `RO28`, `RO31` and `RO33`
  thread a paragraph between the images in the same row.

  FOUR BLOCKS: the tanning, the cut, the stitch, and what lasts. They are OURS
  and flagged `processNotes`, so `npm run launch-check` refuses while they
  stand — HIS making statement is still the first prose on the page, up in the
  paired surface, untouched and unmoved.

  ZIPPED with the cut-outs rather than queued after them, so consecutive mixed
  rows carry different kinds instead of three objects and then four paragraphs.
*/
const notesVoice = voiceState(settings, locale, true);
const notes = (settings.processNotes ?? [])
  .map((n) => noteBlock(n, (f) => pick(f, locale), SHOW_UNAPPROVED_MARKS && isMarked(notesVoice), t(locale, "draftNotice")))
  .filter((b): b is MosaicBlock => b !== null);

const mosaicBlocks: MosaicBlock[] = weaveMosaic(
  mosaic,
  zip(
    notes,
    mine.map((c) =>
      productBlock(c, c.garment?.slug ? localePath(locale, `creature/${c.garment.slug}`) : null),
    ),
  ),
);
---

<Base
  locale={locale}
  shopChrome={false}
  title={t(locale, "process")}
  description={making ?? undefined}
  image={first ? imageUrl(first.poster, 1200) : null}
>
  {/*
    The formula, inside, in the site's one display size. Then his own account
    of the work, once, and then only photographs.
  */}
  {/*
    THE TITLE TAKES THE ROOM TWO LINES NEED (2026-08-13, section 108). It held
    50svh — 450px at 1440, 540px at 1920 — for a display line and a label, and
    `justify-end` put all of that above them, so the page opened on a third of a
    screen of nothing. Same correction section 105 made to the home page's
    headings, applied to the one page it missed.
  */}
  <TextSurface arrive class="flex flex-col gap-u3 pt-u13 pb-u8">
    <h1 class="type-display text-display">{t(locale, "processTitle")}</h1>
    <p class="type-label text-label">{t(locale, "process")}</p>
  </TextSurface>

  {/*
    COMPOSED, NOT ALONE ON A SCREEN (2026-08-11, section 82). His account of the
    making shares a screen with a photograph rather than sitting on a flat one
    above it — a passage in the body register is never alone on this site.

    Its frame comes from the gallery rather than from the making sequence
    (section 85): an anchor is lifted out of the descent below, and the eight
    frames of the work must stay whole and in order.
  */}
  {/*
    ONE COLUMN, BOTH PASSAGES, ONE PHOTOGRAPH (2026-08-13, section 108), which
    is what /about was rebuilt into on 2026-08-13 and what both references do.

    This page had TWO paired surfaces in a row: his three making lines beside
    one frame, then our stages paragraph beside another. Looked at rather than
    reasoned about, each was a handful of lines floating in half a screen —
    the making passage measured about 96px of text in a 561px row, four fifths
    of its half empty — and the page spent two full compositions and two of its
    photographs saying eight lines.

    The reference answer is a NARROW COLUMN CARRYING MANY PARAGRAPHS beside one
    picture, never a paragraph to a screen. So the two passages become one
    column, in the order a reader needs them — what the work is, then what the
    stages are — and the second anchor goes back into the mosaic below, where a
    photograph is doing something.

    THE TWO VOICES STAY SEPARATE INSIDE THE COLUMN. His lines are his and carry
    whatever `Statement` decides; the stages paragraph is OURS, flagged
    `processText`, and keeps its own draft mark directly above itself. Merging
    the layout is not merging the authorship, and the mark has to stay attached
    to the sentence it is about (section 22's rule for /about, same reason).

    ONE PARAGRAPH FOR THE STAGES, still deliberate (section 81): a line per
    photograph would be the caption gallery this page has always refused to be.
    It still sits directly above the eight frames it describes (section 85).
  */}
  <PairedSurface media={pairA ?? null} locale={locale} side="left" priority>
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-u5">
      <Statement text={making} locale={locale} voice={voice} register="body" bare />
      {processText && (
        <>
          {processIsMarked && <p class="placeholder-mark self-start">{t(locale, notice)}</p>}
          <p class:list={["type-body text-body whitespace-pre-line", processIsMarked && "draft-text"]}>
            {processText}
          </p>
        </>
      )}
    </div>
  </PairedSurface>

  {/*
    THE MOSAIC IS ACTUALLY A MOSAIC NOW (2026-08-12, section 115).

    WHAT IT WAS: `grid-cols-2`, every cell a viewport-height crop, alternating
    tall and short on a phone, landscape frames spanning both columns. Two
    columns of equal width with no white anywhere is a CONTACT SHEET, and the
    variable it was calling `mosaic` had been describing something the page did
    not have since the name was chosen.

    WHAT THE REFERENCE ACTUALLY DOES, from all 77 frames rather than from a
    sentence about them: block widths from 10% to 66% changing every unit, the
    anchor alternating left / right / centre, blocks not top-aligned, columns
    running past each other, and the empty space always to ONE SIDE. `RO20` to
    `RO34` is Rick Owens' own process page and it is the direct analogue of this
    one — the same subject, the same kinds of picture, prose threaded through it.

    NOTHING IS CROPPED. `height="native"` gives each frame its own proportion,
    because the reference's rhythm comes from frames that DIFFER in shape and
    forcing them to a common height is what produced the wall this replaces.

    NO `priority` HERE, unchanged from section 108: the anchor above is this
    page's first photograph and carries it, and two images declaring high fetch
    priority is two images competing for one connection.

    `isLandscape` IS GONE WITH THE OLD GRID. It existed to make a wide frame
    span two equal columns; there are no equal columns now, and a wide frame
    simply lands wherever the cycle puts it — which is the reference's own
    answer, since `RO17` puts a small portrait and a two-thirds landscape in
    consecutive units without either being told about the other.
  */}
  <Mosaic blocks={mosaicBlocks} locale={locale} />

  {/*
    u8, not u13 (2026-08-13, section 108). Thirteen units above and below a
    single 12px link was 510px of empty paper at 1440 — 94% air around the one
    thing on the page asking to be clicked. Section 105 made exactly this
    correction to the home page's heading blocks; this one was missed because
    the page ends here and nobody scrolls past the end to look.
  */}
  <TextSurface class="hairline-h flex flex-col gap-u3 py-u8">
    <a
      href={localePath(locale, "creature")}
      class="type-label text-label self-start hairline-under"
    >
      {t(locale, "allCreatures")}
    </a>
  </TextSurface>
</Base>
